From 7c20e0be53d203a6acf2554eaa87254b70867c0f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hoangvu12 <hggaming91@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 19:45:25 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] A direct link proves which of the person's devices it's from,
 and plugins' calls take it

A direct link's far end was the signaling's word, unproven, so its calls
were served as an unverified peer's and plugins' calls (reviews,
decisions, installs from another device, a plugin's deliveries, service
and agent calls) kept to the relay, whose edge checks a dial's proof.

- Each link now proves its device first, with the proof the relay dial
  already sends the edge: the device's registered signing key over
  "zeron-device-link:v1:{host}:direct-{nonce}:{ts}", the nonce the host's
  for this link (DirectHello: {} -> {device, nonce}, then
  {device, key, signature, ts} -> {device}). The host checks it against the
  account's keys (GET /plugin-keys, kept in <data>/device-keys.json):
  the key is the named device's, not revoked, the signature holds, the
  time is within 5 minutes, and the device is the one the transport says.
  Proven, the link's calls are served as that device's
  (Caller::Peer{device: Some}), as a stamped relay dial's are. Not proven
  (no key yet, a host from before answering unknown method, keys
  unreadable): served as before.
- The account's keys are read again when the copy here is 10 minutes old,
  or lacks the key asked about (at most every 30 s); a revoked key stops
  counting then. With the edge out of reach the copy counts for an hour.
- Roads::for_method sends a call that needs the verified caller
  (needs_verified_caller) down a direct link only once it proved this
  device (Links::proven_client), else the relay. Plugins take Roads now
  (Plugins::set_roads): forward_call (ServePluginCall), ServePluginAgents,
  PluginDeliver (both places) and CheckPluginKey. rpc.rs forward's plugin
  methods follow the same rule.
- Nothing in the check is tied to WebRTC: device_proof (Claim, Verifier,
  check, AccountKeys) and direct_rpc::proven_service / direct_rpc::prove
  work over any byte stream between two devices.

Tests: device_proof's a_claim_proves_the_device_its_key_is_registered_to
(another host, link, device, time, a revoked or unknown key) and
keys_are_read_when_stale_or_missing_and_a_revocation_counts; direct_rpc's
a_link_that_proves_its_device_is_served_as_that_devices (and a host from
before) and plugin_calls_take_a_link_that_proved_this_device.
